<div class="acdt-intro">
  <p>
    Welcome to the comprehensive <strong>GMT to EST Conversion</strong> reference portal. This page provides a dedicated, date-aware guide for calculating time between Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0) and North American Eastern Time (EST, UTC-5 / EDT, UTC-4).
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike static calculators that rely on a single fixed offset, our tool connects directly to the IANA Time Zone Database to resolve seasonal Daylight Saving Time shifts automatically. Whether you need a <a href="#conversion-table">24-hour comparison chart</a>, a <a href="#manual-calc">manual conversion formula</a>, or guidance on <a href="#previous-day">previous-day results</a>, this guide covers every mathematical and practical nuance of transatlantic scheduling. You can also explore our foundational <a href="/time-zone-guide/">Time Zone Guide</a>, compare <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a>, or review our <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">GMT vs UTC</a> analysis.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     HOW GMT AND EASTERN TIME DIFFER
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="how-they-differ">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Longitudinal Baseline</div>
    <h2>How GMT and Eastern Time Differ</h2>
    <p>
      Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) and Eastern Standard Time (EST) differ because of the Earth's physical rotation and longitudinal distance. The Earth completes a 360-degree rotation every 24 hours, meaning every 15 degrees of longitude represents one hour of solar travel. The Prime Meridian (0° longitude) passes through the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London, serving as the international reference line for UTC+0.
    </p>
    <p>
      In contrast, the Eastern Time corridor in North America is centered along the 75th meridian west (75°W longitude). This physical distance places Eastern Standard Time exactly 5 hours behind Greenwich solar time (75° / 15° per hour = 5 hours). During the summer months, North America observes daylight saving time (EDT), advancing clocks by one hour to UTC-4 and temporarily narrowing the gap with GMT to 4 hours.
    </p>
  </div>
  
  <!-- ILLUSTRATION 1: GMT VS EST TIMELINE -->
  <div class="hero-visual" style="max-width: 800px; margin: 24px auto 0;">
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 800 220" width="100%" height="auto" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0c354a 0%, #12455d 100%); border-radius: 8px;">
      <text x="400" y="40" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" fill="#36c7b5" text-anchor="middle">GMT VS EST TIMELINE &amp; OFFSET RELATIONSHIP</text>
