      <!-- GMT Node -->
      <circle cx="200" cy="120" r="12" fill="#36c7b5" stroke="#0c354a" stroke-width="3" />
      <text x="200" y="90" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#f4fbff" text-anchor="middle">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</text>
      <text x="200" y="155" font-family="'Manrope', sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#bdd2dc" text-anchor="middle">UTC+0 Baseline Meridian</text>
      
      <!-- EST Node -->
      <circle cx="600" cy="120" r="12" fill="#8fd7ff" stroke="#0c354a" stroke-width="3" />
      <text x="600" y="90" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#36c7b5" text-anchor="middle">Eastern Standard Time (EST)</text>
      <text x="600" y="155" font-family="'Manrope', sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#bdd2dc" text-anchor="middle">UTC-5 Winter Offset</text>

      <!-- Offset Bracket -->
      <path d="M 200 120 L 200 140 L 600 140 L 600 120" fill="none" stroke="#36c7b5" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.6" />
      <text x="400" y="185" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="bold" fill="#8fd7ff" text-anchor="middle">5-Hour Standard Time Gap (4h in Summer EDT)</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     EST VS EDT: SEASONAL OFFSET & DST TRANSITIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="seasonal-offset">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Daylight Saving Mechanics</div>
    <h2>EST vs EDT: Seasonal Offset &amp; DST Transitions</h2>
    <p>
      A common source of confusion in timezone calculations is treating "EST" as an all-encompassing term for Eastern Time. In standard timekeeping, Eastern Standard Time (EST) strictly refers to the winter offset of <strong>UTC-5</strong>. During warmer months, the region observes Eastern Daylight Time (EDT) at <strong>UTC-4</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul style="margin: 16px 0 0; padding-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      <li><strong>Standard Time Difference (5 Hours)</strong>: For roughly 48 weeks of the year, London and New York are 5 hours apart. In winter, London is on GMT (UTC+0) and New York is on EST (UTC-5). In summer, London is on BST (UTC+1) and New York is on EDT (UTC-4). Both advance by one hour, preserving the standard 5-hour difference.</li>
      <li><strong>DST Transition Mismatch (4 Hours)</strong>: The United States and the United Kingdom change clocks on different dates. During the 2- to 3-week gap in March and the 1-week gap in late October/early November, the time difference narrows temporarily to <strong>4 hours</strong>. London and New York are <strong>never 6 hours apart</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Spring-Forward Transition</strong>: On the second Sunday of March at 2:00 AM EST, clocks jump to 3:00 AM EDT. The 2:00 AM–3:00 AM interval is skipped (a 23-hour day).</li>
      <li><strong>Fall-Back Transition</strong>: On the first Sunday of November at 2:00 AM EDT, clocks turn back to 1:00 AM EST. The 1:00 AM–2:00 AM interval is repeated twice (a 25-hour day).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  
  <!-- Verified 2026 & 2027 DST Schedule Table -->
  <div style="margin-top: 24px;">
    <h3>Verified 2026 &amp; 2027 Daylight Saving Time Dates (US vs UK)</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th>
            <th>US Eastern Time (Spring Forward)</th>
            <th>UK / London (BST Begins)</th>
            <th>UK / London (BST Ends)</th>
            <th>US Eastern Time (Fall Back)</th>
            <th>Mismatch Gap Duration</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2026</strong></td>
            <td>Sun, March 8, 2026<br /><small>(2:00 AM EST → 3:00 AM EDT)</small></td>
            <td>Sun, March 29, 2026<br /><small>(1:00 AM GMT → 2:00 AM BST)</small></td>
            <td>Sun, October 25, 2026<br /><small>(2:00 AM BST → 1:00 AM GMT)</small></td>
            <td>Sun, November 1, 2026<br /><small>(2:00 AM EDT → 1:00 AM EST)</small></td>
            <td>3 weeks in March (4 hrs)<br />1 week in Oct/Nov (4 hrs)</td>
          </tr>
