<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     HOW TO CONVERT GMT TO EST MANUALLY
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="manual-calc">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Manual Formulas</div>
    <h2>How to Convert GMT to EST Manually</h2>
    <p>
      Converting Greenwich Mean Time to Eastern Time manually requires applying the correct offset formula based on whether Daylight Saving Time is currently active.
    </p>
    
    <div class="method-grid">
      <div class="method-card">
        <h3>Standard Time (Winter)</h3>
        <code>EST = GMT - 5 Hours</code>
        <p>Active November to March. Subtract 5 from the GMT hour. For example: <strong>14:00 GMT - 5 = 09:00 EST</strong> (9:00 AM EST).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="method-card">
        <h3>Daylight Saving (Summer)</h3>
        <code>EDT = GMT - 4 Hours</code>
        <p>Active March to November. Subtract 4 from the GMT hour. For example: <strong>14:00 GMT - 4 = 10:00 EDT</strong> (10:00 AM EDT).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="method-card">
        <h3>Previous Day Rule</h3>
        <code>If Result &lt; 0: Add 24</code>
        <p>If subtraction yields a negative number, add 24 to find the hour and roll back 1 calendar day. Example: <strong>02:00 GMT - 5 = -3 → 21:00 EST (9 PM Prev Day)</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     WHY PREVIOUS-DAY RESULTS OCCUR
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="previous-day">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Calendar Rollover</div>
    <h2>Why Previous-Day Results Occur</h2>
    <p>
      Because Greenwich Mean Time is situated east of North America, it enters each new calendar day several hours earlier. When midnight strikes in Greenwich (12:00 AM GMT), clocks on the North American East Coast read 7:00 PM EST (or 8:00 PM EDT) on the <em>preceding evening</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This creates a consistent 5-hour window in winter (or 4-hour window in summer) where GMT is on the next calendar day relative to Eastern Time:
    </p>
    <ul style="margin: 16px 0 0; padding-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      <li><strong>12:00 AM GMT (00:00)</strong> converts to <strong>7:00 PM EST</strong> (Previous Day) / <strong>8:00 PM EDT</strong> (Previous Day).</li>
      <li><strong>1:00 AM GMT (01:00)</strong> converts to <strong>8:00 PM EST</strong> (Previous Day) / <strong>9:00 PM EDT</strong> (Previous Day).</li>
      <li><strong>2:00 AM GMT (02:00)</strong> converts to <strong>9:00 PM EST</strong> (Previous Day) / <strong>10:00 PM EDT</strong> (Previous Day).</li>
      <li><strong>3:00 AM GMT (03:00)</strong> converts to <strong>10:00 PM EST</strong> (Previous Day) / <strong>11:00 PM EDT</strong> (Previous Day).</li>
      <li><strong>4:00 AM GMT (04:00)</strong> converts to <strong>11:00 PM EST</strong> (Previous Day) / <strong>12:00 AM EDT (Midnight)</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>5:00 AM GMT (05:00)</strong> reaches <strong>12:00 AM EST (Midnight)</strong>, aligning both regions on the same calendar day.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

